"\section=1"
"\name=ignorerevs"
"\summary=update git repository .git-blame-ignore-revs from history"

namespace ignorerevs
{
  {
    "<options> <dir>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{ignorerevs} [<options>] [<dir>]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{ignorerevs} command updates the \cb{.git-blame-ignore-revs} file
     of a git repository, which lists the commits that \cb{git-blame(1)}
     skips, attributing the lines they touch to the commits before them. Git
     reads the file if \cb{blame.ignoreRevsFile} is set to it and GitHub reads
     it by default.

     The file lists every commit in the history of \cb{HEAD} that only
     changes whitespace (see WHITESPACE below for details), along with the
     commits it already lists. So a commit added by hand, for example, a
     reformat that also moves line breaks, is kept on every update.

     If <dir> is specified, then it should be a directory inside the working
     tree of the repository to update or, with \cb{--all}, the directory to
     search for repositories. Otherwise, the current working directory is
     assumed.

     Each commit is written as its full object name preceded by a comment
     with its subject, oldest first, after a comment that says what the file
     is. A commit added by hand may be written in any form git understands,
     for example, abbreviated, and is written back in full. The command fails
     if the file lists something that is not a commit in the history of
     \cb{HEAD} (for example, after the history was rewritten), listing each
     entry, as well as for a shallow clone, whose history is incomplete. A
     repository without such commits and without the file is left alone.

     The update proceeds the same way as that of \cb{mailmap(1)}:

     \ol|

     \li|Prepare every repository: generate its \cb{.git-blame-ignore-revs}
     and establish that it can be installed, committed, and, if requested,
     pushed. If any repository fails to prepare, then nothing is changed in
     any of them.|

     \li|Print the plan: for each repository whose file is not up to date,
     the numbers of commits in the history, of those that only change
     whitespace, and of those added by hand, where the commit is pushed to,
     and the changes to the file.|

     \li|Ask for confirmation, unless \cb{--yes} is specified.|

     \li|Install each file with a single rename, then commit and, if
     requested, push it.||

     For example:

     \
     $ ignorerevs

     repository:         /src/hello
     history commits:    4
     whitespace commits: 1

     +# Commits that git-blame(1) skips since they only change formatting.
     ...
     +# Indent main
     +f6cff4f0b9e84e07b0ea7d03a91b5147cca59b0f

     update '/src/hello/.git-blame-ignore-revs'? [y/n]: y
     \

     The commit subject is \cb{Update .git-blame-ignore-revs} and its message
     body lists the commits the update adds, for example:

     \
     Update .git-blame-ignore-revs

       add    f6cff4f Indent main
     \

     \h#whitespace|WHITESPACE|

     A commit only changes whitespace if it modifies some files and nothing
     else and each file is the same before and after the commit once its
     lines are normalized: the leading and trailing whitespace of each line is
     removed, each run of whitespace within it is replaced with a single
     space, and blank lines are dropped. A commit that changes indentation,
     trailing whitespace, the amount of whitespace between words, blank
     lines, or line endings qualifies. A commit that adds or removes a space
     between two words, adds, removes, or renames a file, or only changes the
     mode of a file does not.

     Merges are not considered and neither are commits that change a file in
     which whitespace is significant: YAML, Python, Markdown,
     reStructuredText, makefiles, and patches.
     "
  }

  class options
  {
    "\h|OPTIONS|"

    bool --all|-a
    {
      "Operate on every repository found under \i{dir}. Without this option,
       the repository \i{dir} is in is operated on. The search does not
       descend into a repository working tree, so nested repositories
       (submodules, repositories checked out inside another one) are never
       visited. Linked worktrees are skipped as well, since their repository
       is visited through its main working tree. Hidden directories are
       searched."
    };

    bool --no-commit
    {
      "Don't commit the updated \cb{.git-blame-ignore-revs}. By default, it is
       committed and only it is, whatever else is staged."
    };

    bool --push
    {
      "Push the commit to the upstream of the current branch. To push exactly
       this commit, the branch must not be ahead of its upstream before the
       update. If the push fails, then the commit is kept and the rest of the
       repositories are still updated."
    };

    bool --show-push
    {
      "Print the push command without executing it. This allows examining
       the commit before pushing it. Note that the command is printed to
       \cb{stdout}."
    };

    bool --print-only
    {
      "Print to \cb{stdout} what would be done without actually doing
       anything."
    };

    bool --yes|-y
    {
      "Don't prompt for confirmation before updating
       \cb{.git-blame-ignore-revs}."
    };

    bool --help
    {
      "Print usage information and exit."
    };

    bool --version
    {
      "Print version and exit."
    };
  };

  "
  \h|EXIT STATUS|

  Non-zero exit status is returned in case of an error, including when the
  update is not confirmed. Nothing is changed in any repository if the error
  is diagnosed before the confirmation.
  "
}
